WEBVTT

00:00:06.313 --> 00:00:09.773
Two visitors share one pass design, but each pass

00:00:09.773 --> 00:00:11.609
keeps its own visit count.

00:00:11.609 --> 00:00:15.423
DeskPass is the class: one definition of the state and

00:00:15.423 --> 00:00:17.188
behavior each object has.

00:00:32.993 --> 00:00:35.916
Owner and visits are instance fields, stored

00:00:35.916 --> 00:00:37.643
separately in each object.

00:00:37.643 --> 00:00:41.229
The constructor has the class name and no return type.

00:00:41.229 --> 00:00:42.956
It initializes a new pass.

00:00:42.956 --> 00:00:46.211
This refers to the current object, separating its

00:00:46.211 --> 00:00:48.868
owner field from the matching parameter.

00:00:49.169 --> 00:00:52.677
Visit is an instance method: it updates the object

00:00:52.677 --> 00:00:53.939
used for the call.

00:00:53.939 --> 00:00:56.254
Each new creates a separate pass.

00:00:56.254 --> 00:00:59.972
Trace the calls below and predict both printed lines,

00:00:59.972 --> 00:01:00.744
then pause.

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:09.491
Maya's pass receives two visits; Luis's receives one.

00:01:09.491 --> 00:01:13.213
Updating the first pass leaves the second unchanged.

00:01:13.213 --> 00:01:16.863
One method definition applies the same rule to both

00:01:16.863 --> 00:01:20.298
objects, so we avoid copying its implementation.

00:01:20.351 --> 00:01:23.475
Add one more call to second's visit method before

00:01:23.475 --> 00:01:24.049
printing.

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:27.363
Which line changes, and why does the other pass keep

00:01:27.363 --> 00:01:28.001
its count?
